    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4bcb66e2_c99f157b",
        "filename": "ssh/keys.go",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 374,
      "author": {
        "id": 8905
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-04-14T18:22:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "yeah :/\n\nI spent a bit of time see if I could just use the *KeyMsg structs here since at least for rsa and dsa keys they\u0027re basically equivalent, but the problem was that the struct tags on the Type parameter of the *KeyMsg types aren\u0027t present in cert keys.\n\neg. if I try something like the following in parseRSACert in server.go\n\n\nvar w rsaKeyMsg\nif err :\u003d ssh.Unmarshal(cert.Key.Marshal(), \u0026w); err !\u003d nil { ... }\n\n\nunmarshal err\u0027s b/c when the certs get marshal\u0027d, their keys don\u0027t contain the struct tags.\n\nquestion: do you think I could get away with adding a struct tag to the various key marshal types?  eg\n\nvar dsaPublicKey struct {\n  Name string `sshtype:\"17,25\"`\n  P, Q, G, Y *big.Int\n}\n\nfunc (k *dsaPublicKey) Marshal() []byte {\n  w :\u003d dsaPublicKey{\n    Name: k.Type(), \n    ...\n  }\n  return Marshal(\u0026w)\n}\n\ndoes that struct tag translate to anything on the wire, or is that all internal to go? if it\u0027s just internal to go, then I might be able to get away with not exporting them.\n\nI\u0027m going to test that. that would make this much cleaner.",
      "parentUuid": "4bcb66e2_a632c6d0",
      "revId": "3073400165a794e8b234e60caaf027d08cbe627c",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
